peer_feedback_assignment_1:
  issue_number: 50
  issue_owner: carmine-montaperto
  issue_ok: yes
  issue_status: OK
  overall_comments:
    positive: |-
      The student applied weighed.mean() appropriately throughout the analysis
      for survey weights which shows understanding about raw averages vs weighted averages.
      ATUS microdata does not perfectly represented every population subgroup, so raw
      averages would over or under them based on the sample collected.In general, the code
      is clean and easy to understand and the rendered html while somewhat bland, it's well-organized.
    negative: Practically speaking, the student would have seen the biggest grade
      improvement by prioritizing Task 6 and Task 7 over some of the extra polishing
      applied to Tasks 4 and 5. For instance, the time spent adding bar chart visualizations
      to Task 5 questions (which weren't required) might have been better spent on
      the required Task 6 graphs.
    advice: Strenghtening the the written communication by picking up the unpaid labor
      thread intro and carrying it out through the analysis and conclusion would help
      the whole analysis feel more thorough and cohesive. Some of the vague terms
      used such as "broader social disruptions" could be more impactful if replaced
      by direct references to COVID-19. Finally the conclusion could be improved by
      synthetizing findings rather than restating observations.
  scores:
    Written Communication:
      score: 6.0
      comment: The report reads more like a well-organized homework submission than 
      an analytical report. The writing is mostly restatements of what the tables 
      already show and lacks the narrative flow described in the "great" category.
    Project Skeleton:
      score: 5.0
      comment: Tasks 1-5 are completed, but tasks 6 and 7 are completely missing.
      The work done on task 4 and 5 are well executed which keeps the submission
      out of the bottom tier.
    Tables & Document Presentation:
      score: 6.0
      comment: The tables are present, but fairly plain. There is no color formatting,
      conditional highlighting, etc. Task 5.3 table has very large raw weighted-hour
      numbers alongside percentages and it is difficult to interpret these in context.
      Solid and clean, but lacking the extra polish and creativity that would move
      it into the great range.
    Data Visualization:
      score: 4.0
      comment: Visualizations are present for some tasks, and the correct types are
      generally chosen, but task 6 is entirely missing which was specifically for
      graph-answers. The plots that are present are essentially all of exploratory
      quality. The bar charts in Tasks 5.1 and 5.2 are the same color with no fill
      differentiation, making them feel sparse. The figures are slightly above ggplot2
      defaults, but missing task 6 keeps the score low here.
    Exploratory Data Analysis:
      score: 10.0
      comment: DEFAULT SCORE
    Code Quality:
      score: 7.0
      comment: The code is solid, readable and largely idiomatic, but there is a near-total
      absence of comments throughout. The code is well-structured and deliberate,
      for example the join logic which shows right-joins and inner-joins when appropriate.
      More commentary would make the reader understand certain decisions such as
      the use of stop() which deviates from the professor's provided code.
    Data Preparation:
      score: 10.0
      comment: DEFAULT SCORE
    Analysis and Findings:
      score: 5.0
      comment: The main reason is that half of the work is missing. The analyses that
      are present are sound, such as the survey weights, join logic and numeral 
      results that pass the "sniff test", but task 6 & 7 are completely missing.
      For the text describing the results, they tend to be mostly descriptive vs
      insightful. For example, no findings connect back to the stated goal of understanding
      unpaid labor, which was mentioned in the introduction.
    Extra Credit:
      score: 0.0
      comment: None of the extra credit opportunities were present.
  mp_number: 2.0
peer_feedback_assignment_2:
  issue_number: 53
  issue_owner: khushwant-khatri590
  issue_ok: yes
  issue_status: OK
  overall_comments:
    positive: There are a couple of things I'd like to note. The third category in
      task 7 seems deliberately chosen to represent himself in the analysis and the
      graphs are all very polished and professional.
    negative: The employment status case_match has a coding error. TELFS codes 1-5
      don't map to what the codebook specifies. For example, code 5 is "Not in Labor
      Force" not "Retired", yet the retirement analysis relies on this variable. I
      would also check the activity_location mapping since there seems to be certain
      valid locations missing.
    advice: The student did a great job here! I would recommend for the student to
      check the code a little closer to avoid any critical issues in the integrity
      of the results shown.
  scores:
    Written Communication:
      score: 6.0
      comment: The writing is clean and it's organized, with an intro that sets up
        the 3 demographics to be researched. The score is due to the content of the
        text which is usually very high-level and only descriptive. It doesn't provide
        any insights into why the results are as they are. For example, "Education
        workers spend most of their summer time on sleep and leisure activities" is
        simply describing what the chart shows, not interpreting why or what it means.
        There doesn't seem to be an overarching narrative, but rather answers to individual
        questions.
    Project Skeleton:
      score: 9.0
      comment: 'The skeleton is great! I didn''t award it a 10 due to only a couple
        of things: the join-design flowchart exercise in task 2 which is described
        in general terms rather than worked through concretely for the two specific
        questions asked (sleeping hours and female participants with no time alone)
        and, in task 7, I couldn''t find the fraction of people in each demographic
        hitting the target level, which was a requirement.'
    Tables & Document Presentation:
      score: 7.0
      comment: The table of task 5.5 feels a little too wide and too busy with some
        columns that don't feel relevant or match the subtitle, for example, "Security
        Procedures Related to Consumer Purchases". The table in task 7 only shows
        one or two activities for college undergraduates and stay-at-home dads which
        limits their usefulness. No use of more advanced gt features such as color
        formatting or conditional highlighting.
    Data Visualization:
      score: 7.0
      comment: The "bump plot", which was a requirement, was exchanged for a bar chart.
        The Task 5 bar charts added alongside tables are fairly plain and add limited
        value beyond what the tables already show. I also didn't ggplot2 which has
        more advanced elements that could've made the score be a little higher.
    Exploratory Data Analysis:
      score: 10.0
      comment: DEFAULT SCORE
    Code Quality:
      score: 7.0
      comment: There are very little comments throughout the document. The activity_location
        mapping in the updated function is noticeably incomplete. load_atus_data is
        redefined twice in the document without explanation, which is slightly confusing
        and redundant. The overall code is solid, but there are key factors keeping
        it from a higher score.
    Data Preparation:
      score: 10.0
      comment: DEFAULT SCORE
    Analysis and Findings:
      score: 7.0
      comment: All analyses are present and the results generally pass the sniff test.
        The Task 5.4 transit activities table appears to pull from a "Vehicle" location
        category that conflates many transportation types rather than specifically
        filtering for bus, train, subway, boat, or ferry as the question asks. As
        mentioned before, task 7 is missing the calculation of what fraction of each
        demographic actually hits the target level and the interpretive text throughout
        the document is more descriptive than insightful.
    Extra Credit:
      score: 2.0
      comment: The student completed the relationship diagram in dbdiagram.
  mp_number: 2.0
peer_feedback_assignment_3:
  issue_number: 67
  issue_owner: jalfred259806
  issue_ok: yes
  issue_status: OK
  overall_comments:
    positive: The ring visualization was the most creative and advanced visualization
      in the document. I think the coord_polar() used to apply the dark background
      theme was a cool move to try to assimilate the Apple Watch activity ring.
    negative: The most impactful area for improvement was analytical accuracy and
      self-review. This is a very important habit to build particularly once you are
      in a professional environment.
    advice: 'I would prioritize the analytical errors which would improve the report
      significantly. Debug the sleep calculation by adding parentheses to fix the
      operator precedence bug: (pull(avg) / 60) |> round(2) instead of pull(avg) /
      60 |> round(2). Fix the TELFS employment status mapping to match the ATUS codebook
      correctly, which will also fix the implausible retirement percentage. Fix the
      college_enrolled logic to filter for == "Yes" rather than non-NA values.'
  scores:
    Written Communication:
      score: 4.0
      comment: The text after every graph and table is very thin and generic. No insights
        are provided. Several Task 6 answers are factually misaligned with the questions
        asked, for instance, Graph 1 is supposed to be about age but shows marital
        status, and Graph 3 is supposed to be about college students by age but also
        shows marital status.The document contains placeholder text. The conclusion
        is very brief and generic, offering no specific findings or synthesis.
    Project Skeleton:
      score: 4.0
      comment: Task 5 has a copy-paste error. Question 3 "Where do high-earning Americans
        spend their time?" is a duplicate of Question 1's code and output, not a new
        analysis. The actual high-earner location analysis appears under Question
        2's heading instead. The retirement percentage (6.5%) and average sleep (2.6
        hours) are clearly implausible, indicating significant coding errors. The
        submission has the appearance of completeness but has too many fundamental
        analytical errors to score higher.
    Tables & Document Presentation:
      score: 2.0
      comment: The tables are unformatted with no headers, subtitles, source notes,
        etc. The table in task 5 with the copy-paste error has the wrong information.
        There are no age groups in task 5.5. The overall document feels unpolished
        and incomplete, with multiple presentation errors.
    Data Visualization:
      score: 4.0
      comment: Graph 1 shows household time by marital status instead of age. Graph
        2 shows top activity codes for all employed workers rather than education
        workers in summer months specifically. Graph 3 shows educational time by marital
        status instead of by age among enrolled students.The bump plot (Graph 4) uses
        marital status as the x-axis rather than age or income as the assignment specified,
        and the activity labels are raw level3 codes rather than readable names, making
        it nearly uninterpretable. Although the student added a ring visualization
        at the end, there are fundamental analytical errors in multiple graphs that
        are too significant.
    Exploratory Data Analysis:
      score: 10.0
      comment: DEFAULT SCORE
    Code Quality:
      score: 4.0
      comment: The sleep calculation has an operator precedence error (pull(avg) /
        60 |> round(2)) since the pipe takes precedence over division, producing the
        result of 2.6 hours average. Task 5.5 and several Task 6 graphs join on married
        instead of age, suggesting variables were copied from previous code blocks
        without being updated for the new question. The Task 7 code feels unformatted
        with several blocks written as single long lines with no line breaks, making
        them very hard to read.
    Data Preparation:
      score: 10.0
      comment: DEFAULT SCORE
    Analysis and Findings:
      score: 2.0
      comment: As mentioned before, there are findings that do not pass the sniff
        test (2.6 hours of average sleep per night and 6.5% retirement rate). The
        copy and paste error in task 5 causes all of the results to be wrong as well.
        Survey weights are applied inconsistently with some analyses use them correctly
        while others ignore them entirely.
    Extra Credit:
      score: 2.0
      comment: The student worked on the ring visualizations, but the "Typical" data
        is based on working professionals only rather than the required Target Demographic
        3, and the "You" data is hardcoded rather than reflecting actual personal
        time use. The visualization also only covers three activity categories, which
        is quite limited. This is why I award half of the 4 possible points.
  mp_number: 2.0
evaluator: raulsolanavarro

